---
mean — Mean of a variable                                              data · tier 1 · since 0.1.0 · 1.0 · closed form

IN BRIEF
    - `d.mean(wage)`: the mean of a variable, one value.
    - `d.mean(wage, by: year)`: a table with the mean of each group.
    - `d.mean(wage, weights: hours)`: the weighted form.
    - `d.aggregate(m = mean(wage), by: year)` or `d.add(m = mean(wage), by: year)`: inside a definition, the
      mean of the frame or of each group.

USAGE
    data.mean(var, by: Vars, weights: Var, weight_type: frequency | analytic | sampling = sampling)

DESCRIPTION
    The arithmetic mean of the non-missing values of `var`. Missing values are skipped with a warning that
    gives their count; a column with no value left, or none at all, gives `NA` with a warning. On a Bool
    column the mean is the share of `true` values, the commonest summary of a treatment indicator. An
    infinite value gives `Inf` or `-Inf`; `Inf` and `-Inf` together give `NA` with a warning.

    Unlike R's `mean`, which needs `na.rm = TRUE`, missing values never make the result `NA` silently: they
    are skipped and counted. With `by:` the result is a table with one row per group, in key order with `NA`
    last; with `weights:` the mean is Σwx / Σw, whatever `weight_type:` says. Inside `add` the same function
    is a reducer: `add(dev = wage - mean(wage), by: region)` takes group means.

ARGUMENTS
    data          Frame. The frame.
    var           Var. The variable, which must be Bool, Int or Float.
    by:           Vars. Repeat the computation within each group; plots draw one panel per group.             (shared)
    weights:      Var. Weight variable.                                                                       (shared)
    weight_type:  frequency | analytic | sampling = sampling. Stata's fweight, aweight and pweight. In 1.0
                  the tests refuse `weights:` with `weight_type: sampling` (Z0411) and point to regression
                  with robust errors.                                                                         (shared)

VALUE
    A `Float`; a `Table` with `by:`.

MISSING VALUES
    Missing values are skipped, and a warning counts the rows skipped. A variable with no value left gives
    `NA`, with a warning.

DETAILS
    The sum is a compensated (Neumaier) sum taken in row order, divided by the count and corrected once by
    the mean of the deviations, so the result is within a rounding of the exact mean whatever the magnitude
    of the values, and the same bits on every target.

WARNINGS AND NOTES
    W0201   missing values skipped, with the count; all missing, or no rows at all, gives NA
    W0204   Inf and -Inf both present, so the result is NA
    Z0101   no such variable in the frame
    Z0102   a variable that differs only in case from the name written
    Z0401   the variable is not Bool, Int or Float

A COMMON MISTAKE
    Giving an expression where the variable goes. The variable is named, and a new one is made first:

    > dataset(wages).mean(log(wage))
    error[Z0401]: `var` of `mean` takes a variable of the frame, not an expression
      = help: compute it first and name the new variable: `.add(v = log(wage))` before `.mean(v)`

    Make the variable, then name it: `dataset(wages).add(lwage = log(wage)).mean(lwage)`.

COMING FROM R OR STATA
    Equivalents: R mean().

    Zeff                     R                                            Stata
    d.mean(wage)             mean(d$wage, na.rm = TRUE)                   summarize wage, then display r(mean)
    d.mean(wage, by: year)   tapply(d$wage, d$year, mean, na.rm = TRUE)   tabstat wage, by(year) statistics(mean)

    R's `mean` gives `NA` when a value is missing unless `na.rm = TRUE`; Stata skips missing values and says
    nothing; here they are skipped and the warning counts them. With `weights:` R's form is
    `weighted.mean(d$wage, d$hours, na.rm = TRUE)` and Stata's `summarize wage [aweight = hours]`.

RELATED TASKS
    - For the spread about the mean, use `sd`.
    - For the middle value, which a few extreme values do not move, use `median`.
    - For the usual statistics of several variables at once, use `summary`.
    - To test a mean against a value or another mean, use `t_test`.

ACCURACY
    Closed form.

REFERENCES
    Neumaier (1974), Rundungsfehleranalyse einiger Verfahren zur Summation endlicher Summen, ZAMM 54, 39–51.
    Higham (2002), Accuracy and Stability of Numerical Algorithms, second edition, chapter 4.

SEE ALSO
    sd, summary, describe

EXAMPLES
    // The mean of the wages. The warning counts the rows with no wage.
    > let wages = dataset(wages)
    > wages.mean(wage)
    27.3695
    warning[W0201]: 211 rows with NA in wage were skipped (line 2)

    // One row for each group with `by:`.
    > wages.mean(wage, by: female)
    mean · wage by female

      female     mean
      male     29.189
      female   25.437
    warning[W0201]: 211 rows with NA in wage were skipped (line 3)

    // Weighted by the hours worked.
    > wages.mean(wage, weights: hours)
    27.433
    warning[W0201]: 35 rows with NA in hours were dropped (line 4)
    warning[W0201]: 210 rows with NA in wage were skipped (line 4)

    // Of a variable of true and false, or of 0 and 1, the mean is the share: here the share of union
    // members.
    > wages.mean(union)
    0.318053

    // Inside `add`, the mean of each group on every row of the group: each wage less the mean of its year.
    > wages.add(dev = wage - mean(wage), by: year).select([worker, year, wage, dev]).head(3)
    frame · 3 rows × 4 variables · panel worker × year

          worker   year    wage        dev
      1        1   2015   18.08   -8.93159
      2        1   2016   17.28   -10.5654
      3        1   2017   20.93   -7.51246
    warning[W0201]: 211 rows with NA in wage were skipped (line 6)
